<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>modern-chemical-manufactures</title></head><body><h1>modern-chemical-manufactures</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and …</li><li>Modern Chemical Manufactures — Ammonia, Nitric Acid, Sulphuric Acid, NaOH, Soda Ash and Urea</li><li>Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process</li><li>Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process</li><li>Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell</li><li>Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…</li><li>Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert…</li></ul><h2>Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and …</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and … — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: manufacture of ammonia by haber's process, principle, flow sheet diagram only · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Principle.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Flow sheet diagram only.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process</td></tr><tr><td>• Principle</td></tr><tr><td>• Flow sheet diagram only</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and … (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process, principle, flow sheet diagram only. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Modern Chemical Manufactures — Ammonia, Nitric Acid, Sulphuric Acid, NaOH, Soda Ash and Urea</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Petrochemical industry: feeds from crude oil.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Pharmaceutical manufacturing: GMP standards.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Agrochemicals: fertilizers, pesticides.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Green chemistry: atom economy, waste reduction.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Core point for 01 modern chemical manufactures.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ All atoms are identical in an element. ✅ Isotopes have same protons but different neutrons.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Atoms are indivisible. ✅ Atoms consist of protons, neutrons, and electrons.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Electrons orbit in fixed paths like planets. ✅ Electrons exist in probability clouds (orbitals).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heisenberg uncertainty means we cannot measure position and momentum precisely simultaneously — not that we lack good instruments.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ de Broglie wavelength applies only to light. ✅ All matter has wave nature; detectable mainly for small masses like electrons.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Quantum number l can be any integer. ✅ l ranges from 0 to n−1.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Magnetic quantum number ml determines energy. ✅ ml determines orbital orientation, not energy (in absence of magnetic field).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Spin quantum number ms can be ±½ or any value. ✅ ms is always +½ or −½.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Aufbau principle means filling from bottom to top of the diagram. ✅ Fill lowest-energy orbitals first: 1s→2s→2p→3s→3p→4s→3d→4p→...</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Pauli exclusion allows two electrons per orbital with any spin. ✅ Two electrons in same orbital must have opposite spins.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Hund's rule means pair electrons before spreading. ✅ Electrons fill degenerate orbitals singly first with parallel spins.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ The mole is just a number like dozen. ✅ 1 mole = 6.022×10²³ particles (Avogadro constant) — a bridge between atomic and macroscopic scales.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Molar mass and molecular mass are the same thing. ✅ Molecular mass is in amu; molar mass is in g/mol — numerically equal but different units.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Limiting reagent is the reactant with smallest mass. ✅ Limiting reagent is the one that produces the least product (determined by moles, not mass).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ % yield can exceed 100%. ✅ % yield >100% indicates impure product or incomplete drying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Molarity and molality are the same. ✅ Molarity = mol/L solution (changes with T); Molality = mol/kg solvent (temperature-independent).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ A strong acid completely ionizes in water. ✅ Strong acids (HCl, HNO₃, H₂SO₄) are ~100% ionized; weak acids partially ionize.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ pH can be negative. ✅ pH < 0 is possible for concentrations >1M (e.g., 2M HCl has pH ≈ −0.3).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Kc changes when concentration changes. ✅ Kc is constant at constant temperature; only temperature changes K.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Adding catalyst shifts equilibrium position. ✅ Catalyst speeds both forward and backward reactions equally; no shift in equilibrium.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ High pressure always favors products. ✅ High pressure favors side with fewer gas moles (Le Chatelier).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Exothermic reactions are spontaneous. ✅ Spontaneity depends on ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, not just ΔH.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Endothermic reactions cannot be spontaneous. ✅ Endothermic reactions can be spontaneous if TΔS > ΔH (e.g., ice melting above 0°C).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ ΔH and ΔU are always the same. ✅ ΔH = ΔU + PΔV; they differ when gas moles change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Heat (q) and work (w) are state functions. ✅ q and w are path functions; only ΔU, ΔH, ΔS, ΔG are state functions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Oxidation and reduction happen separately. ✅ Oxidation and reduction always occur together (redox).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Oxidizing agent gets oxidized. ✅ Oxidizing agent accepts electrons and gets reduced.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Reducing agent gets reduced. ✅ Reducing agent donates electrons and gets oxidized.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Faraday's laws apply only to metals. ✅ Faraday's laws apply to any substance deposited/dissolved at electrodes.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Real gases obey PV=nRT at all conditions. ✅ Real gases deviate at high pressure and low temperature.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ van der Waals constants a and b are same for all gases. ✅ a (attraction) and b (volume) are gas-specific.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Surface tension increases with temperature. ✅ Surface tension decreases as temperature increases.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Viscosity decreases with temperature for liquids. ✅ Viscosity of liquids decreases with T; gases increase with T.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Colligative properties depend on nature of solute. ✅ Colligative properties depend only on number of particles, not identity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Raoult's law applies to all solutions. ✅ Raoult's law is exact for ideal solutions; approximate for dilute real solutions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Osmotic pressure is a colligative property. ✅ Yes — π = CRT depends on solute particle concentration.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Atomic structure: From Rutherford's nuclear model through Bohr's quantized orbits to the modern quantum mechanical model, understanding electron configuration using four quantum numbers is fundamental.

Stoichiometry: The mole concept bridges atomic and macroscopic scales; limiting reagent determines theoretical yield; concentration expressed as molarity or molality.

Chemical equilibrium: Dynamic equilibrium has equal forward and backward rates; Kc/Kp are temperature-dependent; Le Chatelier's principle predicts response to stress.

Thermodynamics: First law (energy conservation); enthalpy changes in reactions; Gibbs free energy determines spontaneity; entropy of universe always increases.

Oxidation-reduction: Redox involves electron transfer; oxidation numbers track electron flow; electrochemical cells convert chemical to electrical energy; Faraday's laws quantify electrolysis.

States of matter: Gas laws relate P, V, T, n; kinetic theory explains gas behavior; real gases deviate due to intermolecular forces and molecular volume.

Solutions: Molarity and molality express concentration; colligative properties depend on particle number; Raoult's law describes vapor pressure of ideal solutions.

Chemical bonding: Ionic bonds form from electron transfer; covalent bonds from sharing; VSEPR predicts molecular geometry; hybridization explains bonding orbitals.

Organic chemistry basics: Carbon's tetravalency and catenation enable millions of compounds; functional groups determine reactivity; IUPAC nomenclature provides systematic naming.

Hydrocarbons: Alkanes (CnH2n+2) are saturated; alkenes (CnH2n) have C=C; alkynes (CnH2n-2) have C≡C; aromatic compounds based on benzene ring.

Periodic table: Elements arranged by atomic number; periodic trends in radius, ionization energy, electronegativity; s, p, d, f blocks based on orbital type.

Applied chemistry: Petrochemical industry, polymer science, green chemistry principles; importance of sustainable chemical manufacturing for environmental protection.</p><hr><h2>Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: manufacture of nitric acid by ostwald's process · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: manufacture of sodium hydroxide by diaphragm cell · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay… — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or solvay… · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay… (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert… — Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: fertilizers, chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fertilizers, production of urea with flow-sheet diagram · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Force on moving charge F = q(v×B); on a wire F = BIL sinθ.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Faraday: EMF ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz's law gives the sign (induced current opposes the change in flux).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Self-inductance L = NΦ/I; inductance stores energy U = ½LI². Mutual inductance M couples two coils.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Chemical fertilizers.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Types of chemical fertilizers.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Production of urea with flow-sheet diagram.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Fertilizers</td></tr><tr><td>• Chemical fertilizers</td></tr><tr><td>• Types of chemical fertilizers</td></tr><tr><td>• Production of urea with flow-sheet diagram</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert… (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Fertilizers, chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fertilizers. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and …</td><td>Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process (principle and … (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of ammonia by Haber's process, principle, flow sheet diagram only. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Modern Chemical Manufactures — Ammonia, Nitric Acid, Sulphuric Acid, NaOH, Soda Ash and Urea</td><td>Atomic structure: From Rutherford's nuclear model through Bohr's quantized orbits to the modern quantum mechanical model, understanding electron configuration using four quantum numbers is fundamental.

Stoichiometry: The mole concept bridges atomic and macroscopic scales; limiting reagent determines theoretical yield; concentration expressed as molarity or molality.

Chemical equilibrium: Dynamic equilibrium has equal forward and backward rates; Kc/Kp are temperature-dependent; Le Chatelier's principle predicts response to stress.

Thermodynamics: First law (energy conservation); enthalpy changes in reactions; Gibbs free energy determines spontaneity; entropy of universe always increases.

Oxidation-reduction: Redox involves electron transfer; oxidation numbers track electron flow; electrochemical cells convert chemical to electrical energy; Faraday's laws quantify electrolysis.

States of matter: Gas laws relate P, V, T, n; kinetic theory explains gas behavior; real gases deviate due to intermolecular forces and molecular volume.

Solutions: Molarity and molality express concentration; colligative properties depend on particle number; Raoult's law describes vapor pressure of ideal solutions.

Chemical bonding: Ionic bonds form from electron transfer; covalent bonds from sharing; VSEPR predicts molecular geometry; hybridization explains bonding orbitals.

Organic chemistry basics: Carbon's tetravalency and catenation enable millions of compounds; functional groups determine reactivity; IUPAC nomenclature provides systematic naming.

Hydrocarbons: Alkanes (CnH2n+2) are saturated; alkenes (CnH2n) have C=C; alkynes (CnH2n-2) have C≡C; aromatic compounds based on benzene ring.

Periodic table: Elements arranged by atomic number; periodic trends in radius, ionization energy, electronegativity; s, p, d, f blocks based on orbital type.

Applied chemistry: Petrochemical industry, polymer science, green chemistry principles; importance of sustainable chemical manufacturing for environmental protection.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process</td><td>Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of nitric acid by Ostwald's process. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process</td><td>Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sulphuric acid by contact process. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell</td><td>Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sodium hydroxide by Diaphragm Cell. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…</td><td>Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay… (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Manufacture of sodium carbonate by ammonia soda or Solvay…. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert…</td><td>Fertilizers: chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fert… (Modern Chemical Manufactures, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Fertilizers, chemical fertilizers, types of chemical fertilizers. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Modern Chemical Manufactures.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Chemical equilibrium is dynamic: forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, so macroscopic concentrations remain constant, not necessarily equal. The equilibrium constant describes composition at a specified temperature, not the speed of reaching equilibrium.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>The introductory concentration expression is an approximation to activities. Pure solids and liquids are omitted from the usual equilibrium expression; changing temperature changes K, while a catalyst changes the rate of approach rather than the equilibrium position.</p><h3>Open research question — Circular materials and selective recycling</h3><p>Researchers are working to recover and reuse active battery materials at scale. Real waste streams vary in chemistry and condition, so sorting, safe disassembly, purity, energy use and economic viability all constrain the process.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Modern Chemical Manufactures”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “Circular materials and selective recycling”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-1-chemical-equilibria">OpenStax Chemistry: chemical equilibrium</a></li><li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10480636/">Peer-reviewed review: direct recycling of lithium-ion batteries</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>